{"name":"Tawny Daylily","hex":"#eee4d1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tawny","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a light brown to brownish orange colour; orangey brown tinged with gold."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause (someone or something) to have a light brown to brownish orange colour; to tan, to tawn.","To become a light brown to brownish orange colour; to tan, to tawn."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tawny"},{"term":"daylily","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several perennial plants, of the genus Hemerocallis, that have fleshy roots, grasslike leaves and colourful flowers that bloom for just one day."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/daylily"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hemerocallis fulva","blocks":[{"text":"Hemerocallis fulva, the orange day-lily, tawny daylily, corn lily, tiger daylily, fulvous daylily, ditch lily or Fourth of July lily, is a species of daylily native to Asia. It is very widely grown as an ornamental plant in temperate climates for its showy flowers and ease of cultivation. It is not a true lily in the genus Lilium, but gets its common name from the superficial similarity of its flowers to Lilium and from the fact that each flower lasts only one day."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/H%C3%A9m%C3%A9rocalle_fauve_%28Hemerocallis_fulva%29_en_juin.jpg/330px-H%C3%A9m%C3%A9rocalle_fauve_%28Hemerocallis_fulva%29_en_juin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hemerocallis_fulva","wikidata":"Q1424440"},{"title":"Daylily","blocks":[{"text":"A daylily, day lily or ditch-lily is a flowering plant in the genus Hemerocallis, a member of the family Asphodelaceae, subfamily Hemerocallidoideae, native to Asia. Despite the common name, it is not taxonomically classified in the lily genus. Gardening enthusiasts and horticulturists have long bred Hemerocallis species for their attractive flowers; a select few species of the genus have edible petals, while some are extremely toxic. Thousands of cultivars have been registered by the American Daylily Society, the only internationally recognized registrant according to the International Code of Nomenclature for Cultivated Plants. The plants are perennial, whose common name alludes to its flowers, which typically last about a day."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Hemerocallis_lilioasphodelus.jpg/330px-Hemerocallis_lilioasphodelus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daylily","wikidata":"Q156275"},{"title":"Tawny (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Tawny is a light brown to brownish-orange colour."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Natugle_stor.jpg/330px-Natugle_stor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tawny_(colour)","wikidata":"Q2836489"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Orange_day-lily_in_Tashkent_Botanical_Garden.jpg/500px-Orange_day-lily_in_Tashkent_Botanical_Garden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Orange_day-lily_in_Tashkent_Botanical_Garden.jpg/1280px-Orange_day-lily_in_Tashkent_Botanical_Garden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Orange_day-lily_in_Tashkent_Botanical_Garden.jpg/500px-Orange_day-lily_in_Tashkent_Botanical_Garden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Orange day-lily in Tashkent Botanical Garden","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOrange_day-lily_in_Tashkent_Botanical_Garden.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thu